WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.849 --> 00:00:11.834
"Sir Francis," said Mr.

2
00:00:11.874 --> 00:00:15.956
Fogg quietly, "we will look around for some transport to Allahabad."

3
00:00:17.257 --> 00:00:19.738
"Mr. Fogg, this delay is to your disadvantage!"

4
00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:21.139
said Sir Francis.

5
00:00:21.159 --> 00:00:25.201
"No, Sir Francis, it was foreseen," replied Fogg.

6
00:00:25.221 --> 00:00:27.723
"What!" cried Sir Francis.

7
00:00:27.743 --> 00:00:33.249
"You knew that the railway—" "Not at all," said Mr.

8
00:00:33.289 --> 00:00:37.432
Fogg. "But I knew that some obstacle would come up eventually.

9
00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:39.413
Nothing is lost.

10
00:00:40.574 --> 00:00:42.975
I have two days, which I have gained, to sacrifice.

11
00:00:44.476 --> 00:00:47.878
A steamer leaves Calcutta for Hong Kong at noon on the twenty-fifth.

12
00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:52.501
This is the twenty-second so we shall reach Calcutta in time."

13
00:00:53.942 --> 00:00:57.184
There was nothing Sir Francis could say to such a confident response.

14
00:00:58.828 --> 00:01:01.929
The three men went separate ways to search the village for a vehicle.

15
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:09.292
Unfortunately, while they had been talking with the conductor, the other passengers had already hired all the available

16
00:01:09.352 --> 00:01:11.213
wagons, carriages, and ponies.

17
00:01:12.914 --> 00:01:13.954
Sir Francis and Mr.

18
00:01:13.974 --> 00:01:15.895
Fogg returned to the train with no luck.

19
00:01:17.676 --> 00:01:20.677
Passepartout, however, returned from his search with a suggestion.

20
00:01:20.697 --> 00:01:26.039
"Monsieur, I think I have found transport for us," he said.

21
00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:28.040
"What would that be?"

22
00:01:28.476 --> 00:01:29.117
asked Fogg.

23
00:01:29.137 --> 00:01:30.838
"An elephant!"

24
00:01:31.258 --> 00:01:32.179
said Passepartout.

25
00:01:32.199 --> 00:01:34.841
"It belongs to an Indian man who lives close by."

26
00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:38.383
"Let's go and see the elephant," replied Mr.

27
00:01:38.403 --> 00:01:45.929
Fogg. Passepartout led them to a small hut, near which a tall elephant stood behind a high gate.

28
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.173
The elephant's owner said its name was Kiouni, Mr.

29
00:01:51.193 --> 00:01:55.096
Fogg resolved immediately to hire him and offered ten pounds an hour.

30
00:01:56.908 --> 00:02:00.569
The owner, however, refused and kept refusing every time Mr.

31
00:02:00.629 --> 00:02:01.749
Fogg raised his offer.

32
00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:07.451
When Fogg offered forty pounds an hour, the owner still wouldn't agree.

33
00:02:08.512 --> 00:02:13.173
Mr. Fogg, without getting the least bit upset, proposed to purchase the elephant completely.

34
00:02:14.713 --> 00:02:17.634
He offered one thousand pounds but was refused again.

35
00:02:19.235 --> 00:02:23.116
Passepartout listened in amazement as his master continued to raise the price.

36
00:02:24.576 --> 00:02:28.059
Finally the man agreed to sell the elephant for two thousand pounds.

37
00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:32.023
Mr. Fogg paid with bank notes from the carpetbag.

38
00:02:33.484 --> 00:02:35.846
Kiouni was led out and equipped for the journey.

39
00:02:37.008 --> 00:02:41.131
After hiring a guide and purchasing a few provisions, they set off at a quick pace.

40
00:02:43.754 --> 00:02:49.019
Instead of following the wide, curving path that workers had cleared for the railway, their guide led them

41
00:02:49.059 --> 00:02:50.240
directly through the jungle.

42
00:02:52.532 --> 00:02:56.796
Growls from panthers and the chatter of monkeys occasionally broke the jungle's stillness.

43
00:02:58.357 --> 00:03:00.679
After a day of travelling, they stopped for the night.

44
00:03:01.399 --> 00:03:03.581
The guide lit a fire, and they prepared a supper.

45
00:03:04.682 --> 00:03:07.384
They ate hungrily and were snoring loudly soon after.

46
00:03:08.785 --> 00:03:11.387
The journey started again at six o'clock the next morning.

47
00:03:12.708 --> 00:03:17.332
Kiouni resumed his quick pace, and their guide said they might reach Allahabad that evening.

48
00:03:19.424 --> 00:03:23.145
Fogg and his companions continued without any unpleasant encounters.

49
00:03:23.925 --> 00:03:28.267
The journey seemed on the point of success when the elephant became restless and stopped.

50
00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.007
It was four o'clock.

51
00:03:31.247 --> 00:03:31.908
"What's the matter?"

52
00:03:32.288 --> 00:03:33.308
asked Sir Francis.

53
00:03:33.328 --> 00:03:37.989
"I'm not sure, sir," replied the guide, listening carefully.

54
00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:41.230
A sound was coming through the thick bushes.

55
00:03:42.411 --> 00:03:47.032
The guide jumped down, tied the elephant to a tree, and disappeared into the dense brush.

56
00:03:48.596 --> 00:03:50.618
Mr. Fogg waited patiently without a word.

57
00:03:51.818 --> 00:03:56.722
The sound grew closer—it was a rhythmic concert of human voices and musical instruments.

58
00:03:58.283 --> 00:03:59.484
The guide returned in a rush.

59
00:03:59.504 --> 00:04:03.547
"A procession of Brahmins is coming this way," he said.

60
00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:04.907
"We must hide!"

61
00:04:06.148 --> 00:04:09.491
Through the branches of thick foliage, they watched the procession pass.

62
00:04:10.711 --> 00:04:16.115
Behind a tall leader danced a group of bare-chested priests, their bodies striped with dark red mud or

63
00:04:16.135 --> 00:04:21.821
paint. A young woman followed, led by four men in sumptuous Oriental clothes.

64
00:04:22.781 --> 00:04:25.082
Each was armed with a curved sword at his waist.

65
00:04:26.002 --> 00:04:31.884
The woman's neck, ears, arms, hands, and toes were loaded with jewels, bracelets, and rings.

66
00:04:32.924 --> 00:04:36.985
She seemed weak, as if only half-awake, and two guards held her arms.

67
00:04:38.506 --> 00:04:42.067
Musicians playing tambourines and cymbals followed at the end of the procession.

68
00:04:44.087 --> 00:04:49.454
After the Brahmins had passed, Sir Francis said, "It is a suttee, a voluntary human sacrifice.

69
00:04:50.774 --> 00:04:53.976
The woman we have just seen will be burned alive tomorrow at dawn.

70
00:04:55.457 --> 00:04:58.799
Her husband has died, and she is to pass into the afterlife with him."

71
00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:02.081
"That is true," said the guide.

72
00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:04.942
"But not all suttees are voluntary.

73
00:05:06.243 --> 00:05:08.764
Everyone in Kholby has heard about this suttee.

74
00:05:09.785 --> 00:05:11.286
This young widow is not willing.

75
00:05:12.646 --> 00:05:15.248
She has been drugged so that she won't resist her fate."

76
00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:20.574
The guide climbed onto the neck of Kiouni and prepared to continue their journey.

77
00:05:22.335 --> 00:05:25.036
Mr. Fogg said, "Suppose we save the woman."

78
00:05:26.556 --> 00:05:27.777
"Save the woman, Mr.

79
00:05:27.817 --> 00:05:29.598
Fogg?" said Sir Francis.

80
00:05:29.618 --> 00:05:34.900
"I have 12 extra hours, which I can devote to saving this woman," said Fogg.

81
00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.121
"You are a man of heart!"

82
00:05:37.541 --> 00:05:38.521
said Sir Francis.

83
00:05:38.541 --> 00:05:41.483
"Sometimes," replied Mr.

84
00:05:41.523 --> 00:05:42.563
Fogg quietly.

85
00:05:42.583 --> 00:05:44.584
"When I have the time."

86
00:05:49.472 --> 00:05:50.753
What a day that was!

87
00:05:52.014 --> 00:05:55.778
Riding an elephant through the jungle and finding a young woman about to be burned alive!

88
00:05:56.758 --> 00:06:01.482
Although the majority of Indian widows are not burned, an Indian woman can never marry again if her

89
00:06:01.542 --> 00:06:02.243
husband dies.

90
00:06:04.205 --> 00:06:08.128
According to Indian tradition, she has to shave her head and wear a white sari.

91
00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:11.751
And it is quite common for young girls to be married to older men.

92
00:06:12.392 --> 00:06:16.635
So if a young girl’s husband dies, she will wind up having to spend most of her life

93
00:06:16.815 --> 00:06:17.276
all alone.

94
00:06:18.676 --> 00:06:20.639
Widows are also seen as a bad omen.

95
00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:26.488
They are usually banished from their families, and many of them go to the city of Vrindavan, where

96
00:06:26.528 --> 00:06:28.431
they can work in the city's numerous temples.

97
00:06:29.693 --> 00:06:33.238
Because of this, Vrindavan is sometimes called "the city of widows."
